es, but by Lemma 1 each sunny line can contain at most two points, giving a total capacity of four points – insufficient.  \n\n2. **Horizontal line.**  \n   The possible horizontal lines are \\(y=1,\\;y=2,\\;y=3\\).\n\n   *Case \\(y=1\\):* it covers \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\).  \n   The uncovered set is \\(S=\\{(1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\}\\).  \n   Any line through two points of \\(S\\) has slope \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\) (diagonal), both non‑sunny.  \n   Hence each sunny line can meet \\(S\\) in at most one point, leaving at least one point uncovered.\n\n   *Case \\(y=2\\):* it covers \\((1,2),(2,2)\\).  \n   The uncovered set is \\(S=\\{(1,1),(1,3),(2,1),(3,1)\\}\\).  \n   The only sunny pair is \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\), but \\((1,2)\\) is already on the horizontal line, so any sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(S\\).  \n   Two sunny lines can therefore cover at most two points of \\(S\\), leaving at least two uncovered.\n\n   *Case \\(y=3\\):* it covers only \\((1,3)\\).  \n   The remaining five points must be covered by two sunny lines, which is impossible by Lemma 1.\n\n3. **Diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\).**  \n   The possible diagonals are \\(x+y=2,\\;3,\\;4\\).\n\n   *Case \\(x+y=2\\):* it covers only \\((1,1)\\).  \n   The five remaining points cannot be covered by two sunny lines (Lemma 1).\n\n   *Case \\(x+y=3\\):* it covers \\((1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n   The uncovered set is \\(S=\\{(1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).  \n   The only sunny pair in \\(S\\) is \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\), but \\((1,2)\\) is already on the diagonal, so again each sunny line can meet \\(S\\) in at most one point, leaving at least two points uncovered.\n\n   *Case \\(x+y=4\\):* it covers \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n   The remaining points are \\(S=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \n   Any line through two points of \\(S\\) has slope \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\) (diagonal), all non‑sunny.  \n   Hence each sunny line can contain at most one of these thre