,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\); the uncovered set is  \n  \\(S=\\{(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).  \n  Any line through two points of \\(S\\) has slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore each sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(S\\). Two sunny lines can cover at most two points, leaving one uncovered.\n\n* \\(x=2\\) covers \\((2,1),(2,2)\\); the uncovered set is  \n  \\(S=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\}\\).  \n  The only sunny pair in \\(S\\) is \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)).  \n  After using a sunny line for this pair, the remaining points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\) lie on a vertical line, which is non‑sunny; a second sunny line can contain at most one of them, leaving one point uncovered.\n\n* \\(x=3\\) covers only \\((3,1)\\); five points remain uncovered. By Lemma 1 each sunny line can contain at most two points, so two sunny lines can cover at most four points – insufficient.\n\n*Horizontal lines.*\n\n* \\(y=1\\) covers \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\); the uncovered set is  \n  \\(S=\\{(1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\}\\).  \n  Any line through two points of \\(S\\) has slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); thus each sunny line can meet \\(S\\) in at most one point, leaving one point uncovered.\n\n* \\(y=2\\) covers \\((1,2),(2,2)\\); the uncovered set is  \n  \\(S=\\{(1,1),(1,3),(2,1),(3,1)\\}\\).  \n  No pair of points of \\(S\\) has a sunny slope, so each sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(S\\). Two sunny lines can cover at most two points, leaving two uncovered.\n\n* \\(y=3\\) covers only \\((1,3)\\); five points remain uncovered, which is impossible by Lemma 1.\n\n*Diagonal lines \\(x+y=c\\).*\n\n* \\(x+y=2\\) covers only \\((1,1)\\); five points remain uncovered – impossible.\n\n* \\(x+y=3\\) covers \\((1,2),(2,1)\\); the uncovered set is  \n  \\(S=\\{(1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).  \n  The only sunny pair in \\(S\\) is \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\) (slope \\(1\\)).  \n  Using a sunny line for this pair leaves \\((1,3)\\) and \\((3,1)\\), which are co