\n   \\[\n   \\frac{m(m+1)}2\\le m .\n   \\]\n\n   For a positive integer \\(m\\) this inequality forces \\(m\\le 1\\).  \n   Therefore **at most one** of the \\(n\\) lines can be non\u2011vertical; equivalently, the number of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le 1 .\n   \\]\n\n3. **Existence for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n   \\]\n\n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) has abscissa \\(a\\) and is therefore on the line \\(x=a\\).  \n   All these lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence none of them is sunny.  \n   Thus a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n4. **Existence for \\(k=1\\).**  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\n   \\]\n\n   together with a single sunny line that passes through the only point not yet covered, namely \\((n,1)\\).  \n   Choose, for instance, the line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\); its equation is  \n\n   \\[\n   y-1=\\frac{1}{1-n}\\,(x-n)\\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad (n-1)y+(x-n)=0 .\n   \\]\n\n   Its slope is \\(\\dfrac{1}{1-n}\\), which is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\); hence the line is sunny.  \n   The vertical lines cover all points with abscissa \\(1,\\dots ,n-1\\); the sunny line covers \\((n,1)\\).  \n   Consequently every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines, and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The previous two constructions show that both \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable, while the column argument proves that no configuration can contain two or more sunny lines.  \n   Hence the set of admissible \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1\\}\\) for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff it is **not**