on with \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) satisfies \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and therefore lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \nAll these lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence none of them is sunny.  \nThus a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction with \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\n\\]\n\nand add one sunny line that passes through the unique point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose abscissa is \\(n\\), namely \\((n,1)\\).  \nChoose the line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\); its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny-1=\\frac{1}{1-n}\\,(x-n)\n\\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad\n(n-1)y+(x-n)=0 .\n\\]\n\nIts slope \\(\\dfrac{1}{1-n}\\) is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\); consequently the line is sunny.  \n\nThe vertical lines cover all points whose first coordinate is \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\); the sunny line covers the remaining point \\((n,1)\\).  \nHence every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines, and exactly one of them is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final answer\n\nWe have shown\n\n* no configuration can contain two or more sunny lines (\\(k\\le1\\));\n* configurations with \\(k=0\\) and with \\(k=1\\) exist for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]"

