"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have revised the solution and now provide a complete, rigorous answer.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad \n   C_i=\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}\\;(1\\le i\\le n).\n   \\]\n   The sets \\(C_1,\\dots ,C_n\\) are the \u201ccolumns\u2019\u2019 of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); \\(|C_i|=n+1-i\\).\n\n2. **Upper bound.**  \n   Trivially we cannot have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines because we are allowed only \\(n\\) distinct lines in total. Hence \\(k\\le n\\).\n\n3. **Construction for every \\(k\\).**  \n   *Define the family of \u201cslope\u2011\\(1\\)\u201d lines*  \n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y-1 = (x-i)\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   Each \\(L_i\\) has slope \\(1\\); therefore it is **sunny** (it is not horizontal, not vertical and not of slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   For any point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(a-b+1\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \n   Substituting \\(i=a-b+1\\) into (1) gives\n   \\[\n   b-1 = (a-(a-b+1)) = b-1,\n   \\]\n   so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_{\\,a-b+1}\\).  \n   Consequently the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) **cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\)**.\n\n   *Adjusting the number of sunny lines.*  \n   For a prescribed integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we keep the first \\(k\\) lines of the family (1) and replace the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines by vertical lines covering the columns that are no longer hit.  \n   Precisely, let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\\bigl\\{L_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\bigr\\}\\cup\n                \\bigl\\{\\,x=j\\mid k+1\\le j\\le n\\,\\bigr\\}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   - The lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are sunny.  \n   - The vertical lines \\(x=j\\;(k+1\\le j\\le n)\\) are not sunny.  \n\n   For any column \\(j\\le k\\) the point \\((j,1)\\) belongs to \\(L_j\\); for any column \\(j>k\\) the whole column \\(C_j\\) is covered by the vertical line \\(x=j\\).  \n   Hence every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).  \n   The family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, i.e. it satisfies the required conditions.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The trivial bound \\(k\\le n\\) together with the explicit construction (2) for each \\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n\\}\\) shows that **all** integers between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) are attainable.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nC_i=\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}\\;(1\\le i\\le n).\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal P_n=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}C_i\\) and \\(|C_i|=n+1-i\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Trivial upper bound\n\nWe are required to use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \nSince a sunny line is a line, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines; therefore  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A family of \\(n\\) sunny lines that covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\)\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y-1 = (x-i) \\qquad\\text{(equivalently } y = x-i+1\\text{)} .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) has slope \\(1\\); consequently it is **sunny** (it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\nTake an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(1\\le a-b+1\\le n\\).  \nPutting \\(i=a-b+1\\) in (4) gives  \n\n\\[\ny-1 = (x-i)\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;b-1 = a-(a-b+1)=b-1,\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_{\\,a-b+1}\\).  \nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on one of the lines \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\).\n\nConsequently the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_n:=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and consists entirely of sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Obtaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k:=\\bigl\\{L_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\bigr\\}\n            \\;\\cup\\;\n            \\bigl\\{\\,x=j\\mid k+1\\le j\\le n\\,\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Properties of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).*\n\n* The first part of (6) contains \\(k\\) lines of the form (4); each of them is sunny.\n* The second part consists of the vertical lines \\(x=j\\) for \\(j=k+1,\\dots ,n\\); none of these is sunny.\n* The total number of lines is \\(k+(n-k)=n\\).\n\n*Coverage.*  \nFor a column index \\(j\\le k\\) the point \\((j,1)\\) belongs to the sunny line \\(L_j\\); hence the whole column \\(C_j\\) is covered (every point of \\(C_j\\) lies on some \\(L_i\\) with \\(i\\le j\\le k\\)).  \nFor a column index \\(j>k\\) we have the vertical line \\(x=j\\) in \\(\\mathcal L_k\\), which contains every point of \\(C_j\\).  \n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is contained in at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\nTherefore \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) satisfies the two requirements of the problem and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completion of the proof\n\nFrom (3) we know that no configuration can have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines.  \nThe construction (6) shows that for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines fulfilling the covering condition and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
