--------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A universal covering containing \\(n-1\\) sunny lines\n\nDefine the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV:\\;x=1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) set  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y- (i+1)= -\\frac{i}{i+1}\\bigl(x-1\\bigr)\n\\qquad\\Bigl(\\text{equivalently } y = -\\frac{i}{i+1}x+\\frac{i+2}{i+1}\\Bigr).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Properties of the lines.*\n\n* The slope of \\(S_i\\) is \\(-\\dfrac{i}{i+1}\\).  \n  For every \\(i\\ge 1\\) this number is different from \\(0\\), from \\(\\infty\\) and from \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(S_i\\) is **sunny**.\n* The slopes \\(-\\dfrac{i}{i+1}\\) are pairwise distinct, so the lines \\(S_i\\) are distinct.\n* The line \\(V\\) is not sunny (it is vertical).\n\n*Coverage of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).*\n\nTake an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a=1\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\in V\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\ge 2\\)*, define  \n\n\\[\ni:=a+b-2 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(0\\le i\\le n-2\\), hence \\(i+1\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) and the line \\(S_{i+1}\\) exists.  \nSubstituting \\(x=a\\) into the equation of \\(S_{i+1}\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\ny\n&= -\\frac{i+1}{i+2}\\,a+\\frac{i+3}{i+2}  \\\\\n&= -\\frac{a+b-2+1}{a+b-2+2}\\,a+\\frac{a+b-2+3}{a+b-2+2} \\\\\n&= -\\frac{a+b-1}{a+b}\\,a+\\frac{a+b+1}{a+b}\n   = b .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(S_{i+1}\\).\n\nConsequently every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one line of the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L:=\\{V\\}\\cup\\{S_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines (the \\(S_i\\)\u2019s) and one non\u2011sunny line (\\(V\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Obtaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\))\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n* Keep the vertical line \\(V\\) (non\u201