. The column \\(C_j\\) correctly lists all points with first coordinate \\(j\\). No issue.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Construction for \\(k\\le n-1\\)\n\n#### 2.1 Non‑sunny part\n\n> “Use the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (which is not sunny) together with the vertical lines  \n> \\[\n> x=2,\\;x=3,\\dots ,x=n-k\n> \\tag{1}\n> \\]  \n> (if \\(k=n\\) the list (1) is empty).  \n> These \\(n-k\\) lines cover completely the columns \\(C_1,\\dots ,C_{\\,n-k}\\).”\n\n**Verification:**  \n*Vertical lines are indeed not sunny (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).  \n*The set \\(\\{x=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-k\\}\\) covers exactly the columns \\(C_1,\\dots ,C_{n-k}\\) because each column consists of points with that fixed \\(x\\)-coordinate.  \n*No error here.\n\n#### 2.2 Sunny part – definition of \\(S_t\\)\n\n> “For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le k\\) define the line  \n> \\[\n> S_t:\\; y-\\bigl(k-t+2\\bigr)= -\\frac{t-1}{t}\\,\\bigl(x-1\\bigr).\n> \\tag{2}\n> \\]”\n\n**Verification:** The line is well‑defined for all \\(t\\ge1\\) (denominator \\(t\\neq0\\)). No immediate error in the formula itself.\n\n#### 2.3 Claim about slopes\n\n> “The slope of \\(S_t\\) equals \\(-\\dfrac{t-1}{t}\\); it is never \\(0\\), never undefined and never \\(-1\\), hence every \\(S_t\\) is sunny.”\n\n**Verification:**  \n*The slope is indeed \\(-\\frac{t-1}{t}\\).  \n*When \\(t=1\\) the slope equals \\(-\\frac{0}{1}=0\\). Thus the claim “never \\(0\\)” is false.  \n*Since a line with slope \\(0\\) is horizontal, it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis and therefore **not sunny**.  \n*Consequently the statement “hence every \\(S_t\\) is sunny” is false for \\(t=1\\).  \n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** – the construction includes a non‑sunny line, contradicting the requirement that exactly \\(k\\) of the lines be sunny.\n\n#### 2.4 Distinctness of the \\(S_t\\)\n\n> “The \\(k\\) lines \\(\\{S_t\\}_{t=1}^{k}\\) are pairwise distinct because they have distinct slopes.”\n\n**Verification:**  \n*For \\(t\\ge2\\) the slopes \\(-\\frac{t-1}{t}\\) are indeed distinct.  \n*However, because \\(S_1\\) has slope \\(0\\) while the others have non‑zero slopes, the statement remains true that all slopes are distinct.  \n*Thus the claim of distinctness is technically correct, but it does not rescue the earlier error that \\(S_1\\) is not sunny. No additional error here.\n\n#### 2.5 Coverage of the remaining columns\n\n> “Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) with \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\) (i.e. the point lies in one of the columns that have not been covered by the vertical lines).  \n> Set  \n> \\[\n> t:=a-(n-k).\n> \\tag{3}\n> \\]  \n> Since \\(a\\le n\\) we have \\(1\\le t\\le k\\).  \n> Substituting \\(x=a\\) into the equation of \\(S_t\\) gives  \n> \\[\n> \\begin{aligned}\n> y\n> &=\\bigl(k-t+2\\bigr)-\\frac{t-1}{t}\\,(a-1)\\\\\n> &=\\bigl(k-(a-(n-k))+2\\bigr)-\\frac{a-(n-k)-1}{a-(n-k)}\\,(a-1)\\\\\n> &=b .\n> \\end{aligned}\n> \\]  \n> The last equality follows from the defining relation \\(a+b=n+2-(n-k)\\) that holds for every point of the columns \\(C_{\\,n-k+1},\\dots ,C_n\\). Consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(S_t\\).”\n\n**Verification:**  \n*The choice \\(t=a-(n-k)\\) correctly maps each column \\(a\\) (with \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\)) to a unique index \\(t\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).  \n*The algebraic substitution is performed correctly up to the penultimate line.  \n*The crucial step is the claim that the resulting expression equals \\(b\\). The author justifies it by invoking the “defining relation” \\(a+b=n+2-(n-k)\\).  \n\n**Why the relation is false:**  \nFor points in column \\(C_j\\) with \\(j=n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\) we have \\(a=j\\) and \\(b\\) can be any integer from \\(1\\) up to \\(n+1-j\\). Hence \\(a+b\\) can range from \\(j+1\\) up to \\(n+1\\). The expression \\(n+2-(n-k)=k+2\\) is a **constant**, independent of \\(j\\) and \\(b\\). In general \\(a+b\\neq k+2\\). For example, take \\(n=6\\), \\(k=2\\). Then \\(n-k+1=5\\). In column \\(C_5\\) we have the point \\((5,1)\\) with \\(a+b=6\\), whereas \\(k+2=4\\). The claimed relation fails.  \n\nBecause the equality \\(y=b\\) is not established, the argument that each point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\) lies on the corresponding line \\(S_t\\) is **invalid**.  \n\nMoreover, even if the algebraic identity were correct, each line \\(S_t\\) intersects a given column at **exactly one** point, while a column \\(C_j\\) (for \\(j>n-k\\)) contains **multiple** points (all integer \\(b\\) from \\(1\\) up to \\(n+1-j\\)). Thus a single line cannot cover the whole column. The construction therefore fails to cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** – the coverage claim is false; the construction does not satisfy the problem’s covering requirement.\n\n#### 2.6 Conclusion of the construction\n\n> “Hence the family  \n> \\[\n> \\mathcal L_k:=\\{x=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-k\\}\\cup\\{S_t\\mid 1\\le t\\le k\\}\n> \\tag{4}\n> \\]  \n> consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), and contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.”\n\n**Verification:**  \n*The family indeed contains \\(n\\) distinct lines (as argued).  \n*However, as shown above, the claim that it “covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\)” is false, and the count of sunny lines is also wrong because \\(S_1\\) is not sunny.  \n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** – the conclusion does not follow from the preceding (flawed) arguments.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\n#### 3.1 Step 1 – assigning points on the first column\n\n> “Because none of the lines is vertical, each line meets the vertical line \\(x=1\\) in a unique point.  \n> The points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) on \\(x=1\\) are exactly \\((1,1),(1,2),\\dots ,(1,n)\\); therefore after a suitable renumbering we may assume that \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through \\((1,i)\\) for every \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).”\n\n**Verification:**  \n*Sunny lines cannot be vertical, so each meets \\(x=1\\).  \n*There are exactly \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) on that line, and there are \\(n\\) distinct lines, so each line must pass through a distinct point.  \n*Renumbering to achieve \\(\\ell_i\\) through \\((1,i)\\) is legitimate.  \n*No error.\n\n#### 3.2 Step 2 – the point \\((2,n-1)\\)\n\n> “The point \\((2,n-1)\\) must belong to some line \\(\\ell_{i_2}\\).  \n> The slope of \\(\\ell_{i_2}\\) equals \\(\\dfrac{n-1-i_2}{2-1}=n-1-i_2\\).  \n> Since a sunny line cannot have slope \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\), we must have \\(i_2\\le n-2\\).”\n\n**Verification:**  \n*If \\(\\ell_{i_2}\\) passes through \\((1,i_2)\\) and \\((2,n-1)\\), the slope is indeed \\((n-1-i_2)/(2-1)=n-1-i_2\\).  \n*Slope \\(0\\) would require \\(i_2=n-1\\); slope \\(-1\\) would require \\(i_2=n\\). Both are forbidden, so \\(i_2\\le n-2\\).  \n*Correct.\n\n#### 3.3 Step 3 – general point \\(P_t\\)\n\n> “For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n\\) consider the point  \n> \\[\n> P_t:=\\bigl(t,\\;n+1-t\\bigr)\n> \\]  \n> (the points lying on the hypotenuse of the triangle).  \n> By the same argument as in Step 2, \\(P_t\\) cannot lie on any line \\(\\ell_j\\) with \\(j\\ge n-t+2\\); consequently it lies on a line \\(\\ell_{i_t}\\) with \\(i_t\\le n-t\\).”\n\n**Verification:**  \n*The point \\(P_t\\) indeed satisfies \\(a+b=n+1\\).  \n*The slope of a line \\(\\ell_j\\) (which passes through \\((1,j)\\)) to \\(P_t\\) is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{n+1-t-j}{t-1}\\).  \n*For this slope to be \\(0\\) we need \\(j=n+1-t\\). For it to be \\(-1\\) we need \\(j=n\\). Thus the **only** forbidden indices are \\(j=n+1-t\\) (producing slope \\(0\\)) and \\(j=n\\) (producing slope \\(-1\\)).  \n\n*The claim “cannot lie on any line \\(\\ell_j\\) with \\(j\\ge n-t+2\\)” is **not justified**. For many values of \\(j\\) in that range the slope is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(-1\\). For example, with \\(n=6\\) and \\(t=3\\) we have the forbidden indices \\(j=4\\) (slope \\(0\\)) and \\(j=6\\) (slope \\(-1\\)), but the claim would also forbid \\(j=5\\) even though the slope \\((6+1-3-5)/(3-1)=(-1)/2\\neq0,-1\\).  \n\n*Therefore the derived inequality \\(i_t\\le n-t\\) is **unproved**.  \n\n**Classification:** **Justification Gap** – the step relies on an unproved (and in fact false) claim about which indices are forbidden.\n\n#### 3.4 Step 4 – deriving a contradiction\n\n> “The points \\(P_1,P_2,\\dots ,P_n\\) have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates, so a single line can contain at most one of them.  \n> Hence the \\(n\\) points \\(P_1,\\dots ,P_n\\) require at least \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \n> The inequalities \\(i_t\\le n-t\\) force the indices \\(i_1,i_2,\\dots ,i_n\\) to be pairwise different, and in fact they must be exactly the set \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \n> In particular, the point \\(P_{n-1}=(n-1,2)\\) would have to lie on the line \\(\\ell_1\\), whose slope is \\(0\\) (horizontal), contradicting the assumption that all lines are sunny.”\n\n**Verification:**  \n*The first two sentences are correct: distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates imply distinct lines are needed.  \n*However, the conclusion that the indices must be exactly \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) depends on the previously unproved inequality \\(i_t\\le n-t\\). Since that inequality is not established, the deduction is unsupported.  \n\n**Classification:** **Justification Gap** – the contradiction rests on a faulty premise.\n\n#### 3.5 The case \\(n=3\\)\n\n> “(For \\(n=3\\) the triangle \\(\\mathcal P_3\\) can indeed be covered by three sunny lines, e.g. the three lines through the pairs \\((1,1),(2,2)\\), \\((1,2),(3,1)\\) and \\((1,3),(2,1)\\).)”\n\n**Verification:**  \n*The three lines have slopes \\(1\\), \\(-\\frac12\\), and \\(-2\\) respectively; none are \\(0\\), undefined, or \\(-1\\). Hence they are sunny.  \n*These three lines indeed pass through all points of \\(\\mathcal P_3\\).  \n*No error (though a brief verification would be ideal, the claim is correct).\n\n---\n\n### 4. Overall Assessment\n\n* The **construction** for \\(k\\le n-1\\) fails on two counts: (i) it includes a non‑sunny line (\\(S_1\\)), and (ii) it does **not** cover all points of the required set (the algebraic justification is incorrect and each sunny line can intersect a column in at most one point). These are **critical errors** that invalidate the claim that every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is attainable.\n\n* The **impossibility proof** for \\(k=n\\) (when \\(n\\ge4\\)) contains a **justification gap** in Step 3, where the forbidden indices are misidentified, leading to an unproved inequality that the final contradiction relies upon. Consequently the proof that \\(k=n\\) is impossible for \\(n\\ge4\\) is not rigorous.\n\n* The only part that is correct is the observation that for \\(n=3\\) a configuration with three sunny lines exists.\n\nBecause the central construction is flawed and the impossibility argument is incomplete, the solution does **not** provide a rigorous answer to the problem. Hence the overall verdict is that the solution is invalid."
