allel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line \\(x+y=0\\), which has slope \\(-1\\)).  \n\nThe line \\(L_{1}\\) passes through \\((2,2)\\) (and also through \\((1,1)\\), which is already covered).\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f3 \u2013 Verification  \n\n- **Coverage:**  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1,1)&\\in L_{2}\\cap L_{3}\\cap L_{1},\\\\\n(1,2)&\\in L_{2},\\\\\n(1,3)&\\in L_{2},\\\\\n(2,1)&\\in L_{3},\\\\\n(2,2)&\\in L_{1},\\\\\n(3,1)&\\in L_{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nHence every point of \\(P\\) lies on at least one of the three lines.\n\n- **Distinctness:**  \nThe three lines are \\(x=1\\), \\(y=1\\), and \\(y=x\\); they are pairwise distinct.\n\n- **Number of sunny lines:**  \nOnly \\(L_{1}\\) is sunny; \\(L_{2}\\) and \\(L_{3}\\) are not. Thus exactly \\(k=1\\) sunny line is present.\n\nAll conditions of the problem are satisfied.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{ and }k=1\\text{ a suitable configuration exists, e.g. } \nL_{1}:y=x,\\;L_{2}:x=1,\\;L_{3}:y=1.}\n\\]"
