ost two** points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a sunny line contained three points of \\(\\mathcal P\\), those three points would be a collinear triple, contradicting Lemma 1 because every triple in Lemma 1 lies on a line parallel to a prohibited direction. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3  \nAmong the \\(\\binom{6}{2}=15\\) unordered pairs of points of \\(\\mathcal P\\), the pairs whose connecting line is sunny are exactly  \n\n\\[\n(A,E),\\qquad (B,F),\\qquad (C,D).\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nCompute the slope of each pair:\n\n* Pairs sharing the same \\(x\\) coordinate are vertical → non‑sunny.\n* Pairs sharing the same \\(y\\) coordinate are horizontal → non‑sunny.\n* Pairs with equal sum \\(x+y\\) have slope \\(-1\\) → non‑sunny.\n\nAll remaining three pairs have slopes \\(1\\), \\(-\\tfrac12\\), and \\(-2\\) respectively, none of which correspond to the prohibited directions; therefore they are sunny. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4  \nIf two sunny lines are taken to be two of the three sunny pairs of Lemma 3, the two points left uncovered form the third sunny pair.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe three sunny pairs are pairwise disjoint and together contain all six points of \\(\\mathcal P\\). Selecting any two of them covers four distinct points, leaving precisely the two points of the remaining pair, which is itself sunny. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility of a configuration with exactly two sunny lines\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that three distinct lines \\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\) satisfy the problem conditions and that exactly two of them are sunny. Without loss of generality let \\(L_{1},L_{2}\\) be sunny and \\(L_{3}\\) non‑sunny.\n\nDenote by  \n\n\\[\na=|L_{1}\\cap\\mathcal P|,\\qquad b=|L_{2}\\cap\\mathcal P|\n\\]\n\nthe numbers of points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) lying on the two sunny lines.  \nBy Corollary 2, \\(a,b\\in\\{0,1,2\\}\\).  \nAll six points must be covered, so the non‑sunny line \\(L_{3}\\) contains the remaining \\(6-a-b\\) points.\n\nWe examine all possibilities for \\((a,b)\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  \\(a=b=2\\)\n\nEach sunny line contains two points, hence each must be one of the three sunny pairs of Lemma 3.  \nBy Lemma 4 the two uncovered points constitute the third sunny pair, so \\(L_{3}\\) would also be sunny – contradicting the hypothesis that exactly two lines are sunny.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  \\(\\{a,b\\}=\\{2,1\\}\\)\n\nSuppose \\(a=2\\) and \\(b=1\\) (the other ordering is symmetric).\n\n* The line with two points must be a sunny pair, say \\((A,E)\\).\n* The line with a single point can be any line through that point that is not prohibited; choose any point of \\(\\mathcal P\\setminus\\{A,E\\}\\) for it.\n\nThe three points left for \\(L_{3}\\) must form a non‑sunny triple (Lemma 1).  \nWe check the four possibilities for the single‑point sunny line:\n\n| single point | remaining three points | are they a non‑sunny triple? |\n|--------------|------------------------|------------------------------|\n| \\(B\\)        | \\(C,D,F\\)              | No (no common line)          |\n| \\(C\\)        | \\(B,D,F\\)              | No                           |\n| \\(D\\)        | \\(B,C,F\\)              | No                           |\n| \\(F\\)        | \\(B,C,D\\)              | No                           |\n\nIn none of the cases do the three remaining points coincide with one of the three non‑sunny triples \\(\\{A,B,C\\},\\{A,D,F\\},\\{C,E,F\\}\\). Hence this distribution cannot occur.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  \\(a=b=1\\)\n\nThen \\(L_{3}\\) would have to contain \\(6-1-1=4\\) points, but by Lemma 1 a non‑sunny line can contain at most three points. Impossible.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  One of \\(a,b\\) equals \\(0\\)\n\nIf, say, \\(a=0\\), then the two remaining lines must cover all six points.  \nThe non‑sunny line can contain at most three points, so the other line would need to contain the other three points, forcing it also to be non‑sunny (again by Lemma 1). Consequently we would have at most one sunny line, contradicting the requirement of exactly two.\n\n---\n\nAll possible allocations of points to the three lines lead to a contradiction. Therefore no configuration of three distinct lines can satisfy the covering condition while having exactly two sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, the value }k=2\\text{ is impossible.}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
